WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.440
Christopher Nolan's "Oppenheimer" throws a
lot at the audience — in a good way.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.260
From the significance of horses to the use
of moody black and white scenes, there's more

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.700
to "Oppenheimer" than meets the eye.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.320
At the beginning of the movie, we follow a
young J. Robert Oppenheimer during his studies

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:15.990
in physics across Europe.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.949
While at Cambridge, Oppenheimer is severely
depressed and becomes frustrated with his

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:24.160
highly critical instructor, future Nobel Prize
winner Patrick Blackett.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.150
In an unthinkable fit of anger, he injects
an apple on the professor's desk with deadly

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.150
potassium cyanide.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.989
Oppenheimer quickly regrets the decision and
races back to the classroom to dispose of

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:37.469
the apple, only to find his idol, Niels Bohr,
holding it.

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:40.860
Thankfully, the whole affair ends quietly
without anyone taking a bite.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.500
Though dramatized, this bizarre event is based
in real history.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.370
Oppenheimer's poor mental health at Cambridge
is well documented, and according to various

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.260
reports he did attempt to poison one of Blackett's
apples.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.070
The severity of the actual encounter is debated,
and it wasn't necessarily anything as dangerous

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.070
as cyanide.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.760
The inclusion of Bohr is a fictional addition
from Christopher Nolan, but the kernel of

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.760
the story remains true.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.860
Like "Memento," Christopher Nolan's second
film, some scenes in "Oppenheimer" are in

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.030
color and others are in black and white.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.520
If you're familiar with his past work, you
might assume that the division is being used

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.299
in the same way as the earlier movie — to
differentiate between two different points

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:17.299
in time.

00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:21.000
At first, that holds somewhat true, with the
black and white scenes all taking place after

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.400
the Manhattan Project.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.590
However, the theory doesn't totally hold up.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:28.740
Oppenheimer's hearing is shot in color despite
taking place well after World War II.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.740
By the end of the movie, we even get versions
of the same scenes in both color and black

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:33.740
and white.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.010
The real distinction seems to be that the
black and white scenes show things from the

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:40.939
perspective of Lewis Strauss, the primary
villain of the film in its final act.

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:45.229
His congressional hearing and all of his meetings
are shot in black and white, as are his versions

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:47.509
of different encounters with Oppenheimer.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:51.390
Early in the film, before he's recruited to
lead the Manhattan Project, Oppenheimer's

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.649
primary area of study is black holes and neutron
stars.

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:59.049
At the party where he first meets Jean Tatlock,
he's knee-deep in black hole research, clearly

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:02.110
fascinated by the inherent paradoxes of the
field.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:05.060
When asked, he says that he's studying "how
stars die," adding,

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.719
"The bigger the star, the more dramatic its
demise."

00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:11.550
This line isn't just science, but a grim bit
of foreshadowing for how Oppenheimer's own

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.060
life will end up.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:16.850
After becoming the most famous scientist in
the world for nightmarish reasons, he tries

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.510
to remedy the damage, but fails.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.280
Frustrated by his outspoken opposition to
continued nuclear weapons development, the

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.530
U.S. government effectively blacklists him.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:30.099
The "star" of the country who helped win the
war is ostracized, the gravity of his notoriety

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:31.300
collapsing in on itself.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:35.810
There's a second sad significance to Oppenheimer's
black hole research, which is that several

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.010
of his contemporaries believed he might have
won a Nobel Prize had he continued exploring

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.500
that area of quantum physics.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.930
Instead, he largely abandoned the field and
became much better known for the Manhattan

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:46.930
Project.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.430
Though Oppenheimer was nominated for the award
several times in his life, he never won it.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.160
General Leslie Groves is one of the major
players of "Oppenheimer."

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.370
He recruits Oppenheimer to helm the Manhattan
Project, and is the driving force in finally

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.920
getting him his security clearance.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.930
"This is the most important thing to ever
happen in the history of the world!"

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.020
Additionally, he offers genuine advice to
Oppenheimer when the FBI and other intelligence

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:10.380
folks start poking around his life.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.000
Groves also plays a less overt role as a measure
of time in the film.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.810
Over the course of the Manhattan Project,
we see Groves visibly rise through the ranks.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:21.940
At his first meeting with Oppenheimer, he's
still just a colonel, but he tells the physicist

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:25.150
that he's being promoted to general for his
work on the project.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.180
We then observe Groves as a brigadier general,
and eventually, a major general.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:33.000
We're not told these things, but the ranks
are shown on his uniform, marking the passage

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.610
of time at Los Alamos in a movie that otherwise
does all it can to muddy its chronology.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.709
Groves retired as an honorary lieutenant general
— the rank he held after retirement when

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:44.709
testifying at Oppenheimer's security hearings.

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:48.530
Though he's starred in a ton of major movies
over the years, Cillian Murphy may be best

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:53.239
known today for his leading role of Thomas
Shelby in the British crime drama "Peaky Blinders."

00:03:53.239 --> 00:03:57.300
Set in the wake of World War I, the series
follows a Birmingham street gang as it ascends

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.720
to international prominence under the leadership
of Murphy's character.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.079
J. Robert Oppenheimer is a very different
character than Tommy Shelby.

00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:08.760
However, there is one big similarity between
the two: They both find solace in their relationships

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:09.760
with horses.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.430
In "Peaky Blinders," Tommy is arguably more
comfortable around the animals than he is

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:14.481
around any single human.

00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:18.479
He has a deep love and respect for horses
that helps him occasionally escape from his

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:19.790
stressful, criminal life.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:23.600
In much the same way, Murphy's Oppenheimer
seems happiest when out peacefully in the

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.410
New Mexico desert with a horse.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:30.160
The rest of his life becomes an unceasing
procession of guilt and death after the Manhattan

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.160
Project.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.660
When he's out alone with a horse, however,
Oppenheimer seems to find some tranquility.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.890
This almost certainly isn't an intentional
reference, as it merely reflects a true aspect

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.410
of Oppenheimer's life.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:45.070
But given that Tommy Shelby is probably Murphy's
most famous role, the similarity stands out

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:47.120
as a notable little detail.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.370
After his initial relationship with Jean Tatlock
ends, Oppenheimer marries Kitty.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:55.750
Things go well at first, but life gets tough
after they have their first child, Peter.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:59.889
Oppenheimer is off organizing the early stages
of the Manhattan Project, and he comes home

00:04:59.889 --> 00:05:04.360
one night to find their infant son screaming
and Kitty drunk at the dining room table.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.820
She explains that he's been crying all night.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.750
Distressed and unsure what to do, Oppenheimer
takes his son to the house of Berkeley colleague

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:14.680
and good friend Haakon Chevalier, who watches
over the child for a time.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:19.570
For the rest of his infancy as depicted in
the film, Peter almost never stops screaming.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:23.669
He cries when the family moves into Los Alamos,
and he cries at the dinner table.

00:05:23.669 --> 00:05:27.640
These details are some of the more fictionalized
parts of "Oppenheimer," but they serve a greater

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.130
thematic purpose.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:33.289
After the Manhattan Project, the physicist
continues to be plagued by screaming, albeit

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:36.699
the imagined kind, as he's haunted by the
horrors of his creation.

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:40.190
His children foreshadow all the death his
inventions ultimately bring.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:44.330
"I don't know if we can be trusted with such
a weapon."

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:47.699
One small detail at the end of "Oppenheimer"
is that in addition to his affair with Jean

00:05:47.699 --> 00:05:52.440
Tatlock, Oppenheimer also slept with close
friend Ruth Tolman during his marriage to

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.440
Kitty.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.479
It doesn't affect the story all that much
but is something you might have expected if

00:05:56.479 --> 00:05:58.670
you paid attention earlier in the film.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:00.250
Ruth doesn't get a ton of screen time.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.700
However, she does feature in a few key moments.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.440
In one scene, before Oppenheimer is invited
to join the Manhattan Project, he's in the

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:11.070
room when Ernest Lawrence and Ruth's husband
Richard begin a meeting concerning the operation.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.790
Because he isn't cleared to know about it,
Oppenheimer is asked to leave, and on the

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:17.710
way out he tells Richard to tell Ruth that
he'll be up to visit soon.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.630
At the time, this seems like a harmless comment
to a friend, but it could be read as a hidden

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:22.630
jab.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:26.069
Tolman gets to be in the room while Oppenheimer
doesn't, and we know from earlier scenes that

00:06:26.069 --> 00:06:27.069
he's vindictive.

00:06:27.069 --> 00:06:30.380
Later, there's a conversation between Ruth
and Oppenheimer at a party.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.910
While shorter and less suggestive — mirrors
the one he has with Kitty earlier in the movie.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:38.850
These clues foreshadow their affair, which
is only confirmed at the end.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:42.810
Some of the small details in "Oppenheimer"
are added for dramatic effect, but a surprising

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.570
percentage of them are pulled straight from
the history books.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:49.530
In addition to the encounter where Oppenheimer
attempts to poison his instructor at Cambridge,

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.699
his meeting with Harry S. Truman is pretty
accurate.

00:06:52.699 --> 00:06:56.950
According to the Oppenheimer biography "American
Prometheus," Oppenheimer did confess to feeling

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:00.770
like he had blood on his hands to Truman,
and the president did brush him off like he

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:02.050
does in the film.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:05.990
During the Trinity test, the movie shows Nobel
Prize winner Richard Feynman watching the

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:09.830
explosion without protective glasses, as he
claims that the windshield of his truck will

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:10.830
protect him.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:15.289
This too is true to life, according to Feynman's
own account in his autobiography.

00:07:15.289 --> 00:07:19.140
Additionally, a lot of lines in the movie
were actually spoken by the actual people.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:23.830
For instance, in his Los Alamos speech, Oppenheimer
did say that he regretted that the bomb couldn't

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:25.070
have been used on the Nazis.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:29.840
Though it certainly makes some tweaks, "Oppenheimer"
has a lot of great historical details that

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.650
really add to the effect.

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:35.509
At the beginning of the film, Oppenheimer
has visions of the subatomic world he's studying.

00:07:35.509 --> 00:07:40.110
Niels Bohr speaks about quantum physics like
it's a magical language, asking Oppenheimer,

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:41.270
"Can you hear the music?"

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:45.290
However, these glimpses of a magical world
give way to more nightmarish visions of the

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:46.970
Earth engulfed in flames.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:50.860
During the Trinity test — the first detonation
of an atomic weapon — the two meld into

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:51.860
one.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:55.250
It's easy to miss, but as Oppenheimer stares
at the fire from the Trinity explosion, he

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.990
sees the glistening particles from his earlier
visions.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:02.199
The crystallized fragments blur into the flames,
creating a sickening distortion of the thing

00:08:02.199 --> 00:08:05.009
that once gave him so much excitement to study
science.

00:08:05.009 --> 00:08:09.100
In the wake of Trinity, Oppenheimer never
again sees the beautiful visions from his

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.100
youth.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.789
He only sees horrors, fire, and dread.

00:08:12.789 --> 00:08:16.270
Throughout "Oppenheimer," there are numerous
references to chain reactions.

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:20.389
Nuclear fission explosions operate off the
basic concept of a chain reaction, and at

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:24.160
one point, the Los Alamos scientists believe
there's a chance that an A-bomb detonation

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.900
could start a chain reaction that never stops.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:30.139
There's a greater-than-zero chance before
the Trinity test that the explosion could

00:08:30.139 --> 00:08:33.920
set fire to the entire atmosphere, effectively
destroying the world.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:37.219
It doesn't, but Oppenheimer believes that
the chain reaction he's started will still

00:08:37.219 --> 00:08:38.240
end in Armageddon.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.680
"Now I am become death, the destroyer of worlds."

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.209
Going along with this theme, the whole structure
of the movie is like a chain reaction.

00:08:47.209 --> 00:08:50.100
We see how certain decisions lead to other
moments all at once.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.580
The beginning, middle, and end of Oppenheimer's
life develop in tandem with each other, giving

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.630
the impression of his whole life being a giant
explosion of causality.

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:02.690
Your mileage may vary on where "Oppenheimer"
ranks in the pantheon of Christopher Nolan

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:03.690
movies.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:07.170
However, one fact is indisputable: The movie
feels like the culmination of many of his

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.250
past projects.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:11.950
The heavy focus on quantum physics can be
tracked back to "Interstellar," which was

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:15.960
praised in its day for being more scientifically
accurate than most sci-fi movies.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:20.221
The black and white vs. color dynamic and
non-linear narrative echo "Memento," while

00:09:20.221 --> 00:09:23.560
the fixation with World War II history evokes
"Dunkirk."

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.780
Nolan himself has said that he sees the ending
of "Oppenheimer" as being similar to that

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:30.050
of "Inception," as both leave the audience
with unanswerable questions.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:34.720
And of course, you could compare the history-altering-weapon
storyline of "Tenet" to the real history of

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:36.269
the Manhattan Project.

00:09:36.269 --> 00:09:38.860
Undoubtedly, Nolan still has many movies left
to make.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:42.700
But the sheer scale of "Oppenheimer" puts
it on the top shelf of his filmography.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:46.700
Given the wide range of movies the director
has helmed thus far, it should be particularly

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.610
interesting to see where he goes from here.

